+---
+name: update-brain
+description: >
+  Refresh project CONTENT into NeuroVault brains on the SSD (the brain at
+  D:\NEURO-VAULT-STORAGE\.neurovault). Clones + incrementally re-indexes a
+  project's working folder into its matching brain, and can discover projects
+  that were never imported at all. Two modes: FULL (sweep every brain + scan
+  project-root folders for never-imported projects) or PROJECT (just the one
+  the user is working on). Always shows a read-only staleness report and asks
+  for confirmation before writing. Invoke when the user says "update the brain",
+  "update brain", "refresh my project in the brain", "index this project",
+  "did this project get passed to the brain", or similar. This is CONTENT/data,
+  NOT the app code — updating the app is /update-neurovault-app.
+---
+
+# Update brain (refresh project content into the SSD brains)
+
+The user keeps their projects in working folders (mostly under
+`C:\Users\steli\Documents\Claude_Projects\<Project>`) and mirrors each one into a
+matching **brain** on the SSD (`D:\NEURO-VAULT-STORAGE\.neurovault\brains\<id>\`).
+The mirror is done by `scripts/import_project_vault.py`, which copies the real
+source (skipping `node_modules`, `dist`, `target`, media, lockfiles, etc.),
+writes markdown sidecars NeuroVault indexes, and registers the brain in
+`brains.json`. Re-running it is **incremental** — sha256 means only changed files
+are touched.
+
+This skill wraps that into a safe, confirmed workflow. **Read-only first, write
+only after the user okays it.**
+
+## Model — run it on Sonnet, escalate to Opus only if genuinely stuck
+
+This is a mechanical task — run a script, copy/convert files, report. It does NOT
+need Opus-level reasoning. Run it **automatically on Sonnet**:
+
+1. The orchestrator (whatever model the session is on) handles the **interactive**
+   parts: pick mode, run the read-only `scan.py`, show the staleness report, and
+   get the user's scope confirmation (which stale brains to refresh, which
+   never-imported projects to create).
+2. Dispatch the **execution** (the actual `import_project_vault.py` /
+   `thesis_incremental_refresh.py` runs) to a **Sonnet subagent** via the Agent
+   tool (`subagent_type: general-purpose`, `model: sonnet`). Give it the explicit,
+   already-approved list of `(source, brain_id, name)` imports so it doesn't need
+   to re-ask anything. It runs them, captures each JSON summary, and reports back.
+3. **Escalate to Opus only if the Sonnet subagent hits something genuinely hard** —
+   a script crash it can't diagnose, an encoding/parse failure, an ambiguous
+   brain↔source mapping. It should STOP and report the problem rather than guess;
+   the orchestrator then either handles it directly or spawns an Opus subagent for
+   that one item. Routine imports should never need this.
+
+In short: **Sonnet does the work, Opus is the break-glass.** The user has approved
+this delegation pattern explicitly.
+
+## Preconditions
+
+- The SSD must be plugged in. Verify `D:\NEURO-VAULT-STORAGE\.neurovault\brains.json`
+  exists (if the drive mounts as another letter, pass `--nv-home <letter>:\...`).
+- `python` available (3.10+). The two tools used:
+  - `.claude/skills/update-brain/scan.py` — read-only discovery + staleness.
+  - `scripts/import_project_vault.py` — the actual copy/index (writes).
+- The app can stay running — its vault watcher will index new sidecars as they land.
+
+## Step 1 — Pick the mode
+
+Ask the user (AskUserQuestion) **FULL or PROJECT**:
+- **FULL** — sweep every brain for staleness AND scan project-root folder(s) for
+  projects that were never imported.
+- **PROJECT** — just refresh the single project the user is working on (default to
+  the current working directory's project, but confirm the folder).
+
+---
+
+## Step 2 (PROJECT mode) — one project
+
+1. Determine the project folder. Default guess: the repo/folder the user is in;
+   otherwise ask for the path.
+2. Read-only staleness:
+   ```bash
+   python .claude/skills/update-brain/scan.py --source "<project folder>"
+   ```
+   Parse the `===JSON===` block. It tells you `imported` (bool), the `brain_id`,
+   and `staleness` (`new`, `changed`, `total`).
+3. **Confirm before writing**, naming the project explicitly, e.g.:
+   > "Updating **Makeup Game** will refresh the **makeup-game** brain on the SSD
+   > (+0 new, ~3 changed). Proceed?"
+   - If `imported` is false, say it will **create a new brain** instead.
+   - If `new == 0 and changed == 0`, tell them it's already up to date and ask
+     whether to run anyway (usually no).
+4. On confirmation, import (incremental):
+   ```bash
+   python scripts/import_project_vault.py --source "<project folder>" \
+     --brain-id <brain_id> --name "<Project Name>"
+   ```
+   - For a **new** project omit `--brain-id` (it derives one) and set `--name`.
+   - Add `--active` only if the user wants to switch the app to that brain now.
+5. Report what changed (the importer prints a JSON summary: files copied,
+   converted, etc.).
+
+---
+
+## Step 3 (FULL mode) — sweep + discover
+
+1. Decide which **project-root folders** to scan for never-imported projects.
+   Default root: `C:\Users\steli\Documents\Claude_Projects`. Ask if the user wants
+   to add more roots (multiple `--roots` allowed — e.g. another drive/folder where
+   projects live). Pass each as a separate `--roots`.
+2. Read-only full report:
+   ```bash
+   python .claude/skills/update-brain/scan.py \
+     --roots "C:\Users\steli\Documents\Claude_Projects"
+   # add: --roots "<another root>" ... as needed
+   ```
+   Show the user the human-readable section: which brains are `up to date` vs
+   `STALE (+N new, ~M changed)`, and the `NEW:` never-imported folders. Parse the
+   `===JSON===` block for the actionable lists (`brains[]`, `unimported[]`).
+3. **Confirm scope before writing — show a NUMBERED LIST and let the user exclude.**
+   Present every candidate action as one flat numbered list, stale-refreshes and
+   new-brain-creations together, each line tagged so the user can see which is which.
+   Then ask which numbers to EXCLUDE (the default is "do all of them"). Example:
+
+   ```
+   These projects will be passed into the brain. Tell me any numbers to SKIP
+   (e.g. "2, 4 no"), or just say go to do them all:
+     1. [refresh] ds2                       (+353 new)
+     2. [refresh] water-app                 (+180 new)
+     3. [refresh] megagym-website           (+57 new)
+     ...
+     11. [new]    Model Supermaxxing        (creates a new brain)
+     12. [new]    NATO - Social Media Scraping (creates a new brain)
+     13. [new]    Status Line               (creates a new brain)
+   ```
+
+   Parse the user's exclusions (e.g. "2,4" → drop items 2 and 4) and act on the
+   remaining list.
+
+   **DEFAULT = refresh existing brains only. Creating NEW brains is opt-in.**
+   Never auto-create a brain for a never-imported project just because it showed up
+   in the scan. List the `[new]` candidates separately and ask explicitly whether to
+   create any of them — the default if the user doesn't single them out is to leave
+   them alone. (A project having no brain yet is often deliberate; don't assume the
+   user wants it indexed.)
+
+   **Honor the user's standing exclusions.** Before showing `[new]` candidates, read
+   the master brain's exclusion list at
+   `…\brains\master\vault\07_ALL_PROJECT_BRAINS.md` under the **"Not Project Brains"**
+   heading, and drop any folder named there from the `[new]` list entirely (e.g.
+   `Status Line`, `#SOON`, `.claude`, NeuroVault repo clones). That list is the
+   user's recorded decision about what should NOT be a brain — respect it without
+   asking. The scanner already filters obvious noise; this catches the
+   user-specific ones. The numbered list + opt-in rule is the final veto chance.
+4. For each **stale brain** the user approved, re-import incrementally using its
+   recorded source from the JSON (`source`) and its `brain_id`:
+   ```bash
+   python scripts/import_project_vault.py --source "<recorded source>" \
+     --brain-id <brain_id> --name "<name>"
+   ```
+5. For each **new project** the user approved:
+   ```bash
+   python scripts/import_project_vault.py --source "<folder>" --name "<folder name>"
+   ```
+6. Summarize: which brains refreshed, which were created, totals. Re-run
+   `scan.py` (read-only) at the end if you want to show everything is now green.
+
+---
+
+## Step 4 — OPTIONAL (opt-in): sync the `master` brain
+
+`master` is the hand-curated routing/overview brain. It is NOT a clone of any
+folder, so the importer never touches it — but its inventory drifts when brains are
+added/removed/refreshed. **Only run this if the user asks** ("also update master",
+"sync the directory", "keep master in step"). Default is OFF.
+
+This is **judgment work, not copying** — run it on a capable model (Opus, or
+Sonnet at high effort), NOT the lightweight import worker. It rewrites prose-y
+inventory files, so be conservative and surgical.
+
+Files to keep in step (under `…\brains\master\vault\`):
+- `07_ALL_PROJECT_BRAINS.md` — the canonical inventory **table**
+  (`Brain id | Source | Role | Current index`) + a **"Not Project Brains"** section
+  + an `Updated: <date>` line.
+- `00_BRAIN_DIRECTORY.md` — per-brain detailed entries (Vault id, Purpose, Physical
+  location, "Use this brain for" topics, "Current import status").
+
+How to sync:
+1. Read the live `brains.json` (the authoritative brain list) and, for each project
+   brain, its `_neurovault_index\00_PROJECT_IMPORT_OVERVIEW.md` (source path, file
+   counts) and the date it was refreshed in this run.
+2. For accurate **note/chunk counts**, query the running app rather than guessing:
+   `GET http://127.0.0.1:8765/api/status` (or NeuroVault MCP `status` per brain).
+   If the app isn't reachable, update the structural facts (which brains exist,
+   sources, refresh dates) and leave counts as-is or mark them stale — do not invent
+   numbers.
+3. **Update, don't rewrite:** add rows for new brains, remove rows for deleted ones,
+   refresh sources/dates/stats. **Preserve the user's curated prose** — roles,
+   routing topics, cross-project themes, and especially the **"Not Project Brains"**
+   exclusions. Bump the `Updated:` date.
+4. **Never re-add an excluded folder.** The "Not Project Brains" list is the user's
+   standing decision (e.g. `Status Line`, `#SOON`, `.claude`, the NeuroVault repo
+   clones). Sync keeps that list; it does not delete entries from it.
+5. Show the user a diff/summary of what changed in master before/after writing.
+
+## Special case — the `thesis` brain
+
+`thesis` is mirrored from `C:\Users\steli\Desktop\PwC\uni (thesis)` by a dedicated
+script, `scripts/thesis_incremental_refresh.py`, which also converts `.docx` to
+HTML/markdown. For thesis, prefer that script over the generic importer:
+```bash
+python scripts/thesis_incremental_refresh.py
+```
+The scanner still reports thesis staleness (its source is hard-coded in scan.py),
+but route the actual refresh through the thesis script so the docx conversion runs.
+
+## Notes & gotchas
+
+- **Incremental + safe to re-run.** The importer hashes files; an up-to-date
+  project copies nothing. Running it twice is harmless.
+- **Confirm per the user's rule:** always state "Updating <X> will refresh the <X>
+  brain" before writing, in BOTH modes.
+- **Big deltas** (e.g. a project with hundreds of new files) are fine but will make
+  the app index a lot at once — mention it if the count is large.
+- **Keep scan.py's skip lists in sync** with `scripts/import_project_vault.py`
+  (SKIP_DIRS / SKIP_FILENAMES / SKIP_EXTS) so staleness counts match what the
+  importer would actually copy. They were aligned at creation.
+- **Drive letter:** everything defaults to `D:`. If the SSD mounts elsewhere, pass
+  `--nv-home <letter>:\NEURO-VAULT-STORAGE\.neurovault` to both scripts.
